{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Southwell1999-AIR-LH10-SUB",
  "Source": "W. H. Southwell, \"Omnidirectional mirror design with quarter-wave dielectric stacks,\" Appl. Opt. 38, 5464-5467 (1999). doi:10.1364/AO.38.005464 - Text p. 5465 and captions of Figs. 2 and 5",
  "Notes": "Omnidirectional mirror: single quarter-wave stack (LH)^10 at 10 um, nL 1.7, nH 3.4, on n = 2.4\nSpecification: Reflect both polarisations at all angles 0-90 deg over a common band (omnidirectional band).\nReported: Fig. 2: R vs angle at 9 um (0.9 lambda0), no absorption: both s and p stay between 0.9999 and 1 for all angles 0-90 deg; s rises monotonically to ~1, p dips to a minimum (~0.99992, read off plot) near 70 deg and returns to 1 at 90 deg; at 0 deg R ~0.99996 (read off plot). Fig. 5: R at normal incidence (solid) and p at 85 deg (dashed), 6-20 um; normal-incidence high-R band roughly 7.8-12.2 um, p 85 deg band roughly 7-10.3 um (read off plot); their overlap is the omnidirectional band. Derived from the paper's Eqs. (1)-(5) (not printed numbers): normal-incidence band 7.836-12.164 um (Delta g = (2/pi) asin(1/3) = 0.2163); p-polarised band at 90 deg: centre 8.822 um, Delta g_p = 0.1656, long edge lambda_LONG = 10.283 um; omnidirectional band lambda_SHORT 7.836 to lambda_LONG 10.283 um, B = 27.0 % (consistent with Fig. 3, between the 20 % and 30 % contours at nL 1.7, nH 3.4).\nNotes: AIR (LH)^10 SUB: 20 layers, L next to air. All layers quarter-wave at lambda0 = 10 um (t = lambda0/4n). Non-absorbing constant indices. Angles in air. Eq. (1) as printed lacks the factor 1/2 (lambda_C = (lambda0/2)[cos theta_L + cos theta_H] follows from lambda_C/2 = tL etaL + tH etaH); the derived numbers use the 1/2.\nLayers from Text p. 5465 and captions of Figs. 2 and 5; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours min(Rs, Rp) 0.999937 over 0-89 deg at 9 um; paper R > 0.9999 at all angles at 9 um (Fig. 2).",
